WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
One amino acid, glycine, can carry +1, 0, or −1.

2
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.120
What decides which?

3
00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:11.020
One comparison, made at every proton switch.

4
00:00:14.533 --> 00:00:20.073
A proton switch is a group that holds a hydrogen ion, a proton, or lets it go.

5
00:00:21.693 --> 00:00:22.693
Holding it is protonated.

6
00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:25.673
Letting go is deprotonated.

7
00:00:26.633 --> 00:00:29.253
Each switch has a set point, its pKa.

8
00:00:30.573 --> 00:00:33.613
That is the pH where half the groups hold a proton.

9
00:00:34.953 --> 00:00:36.113
Below it, most hold on.

10
00:00:37.473 --> 00:00:38.513
Above it, most let go.

11
00:00:39.517 --> 00:00:44.697
Each pH unit from the pKa changes the ratio of the two forms tenfold.

12
00:00:45.977 --> 00:00:49.277
One unit above, about 91% percent have let go.

13
00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:55.220
A proton carries +1, so holding it makes a group one charge more positive.

14
00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.660
An acid-type switch, like a carboxyl, goes from 0 to −1.

15
00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:05.820
A base-type switch, like an amino group, goes from +1 to 0.

16
00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.220
So letting go is not always negative.

17
00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.490
Glycine has two backbone switches.

18
00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:19.330
The carboxyl's pKa is about 2.3; the amino group's, about 9.6.

19
00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:23.930
Here is the whole idea: net charge is the sum of the switches.

20
00:01:25.133 --> 00:01:31.993
Your turn. At blood pH, about 7.4, check each switch against its pKa.

21
00:01:33.473 --> 00:01:34.833
What is glycine's net charge?

22
00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:45.673
7.4 is above the carboxyl's pKa, so it lets go: minus −1.

23
00:01:46.933 --> 00:01:50.253
It is below the amino group's, so it holds on: plus +1.

24
00:01:51.493 --> 00:01:55.113
The sum is zero: a zwitterion, with both a plus and a minus.

25
00:01:56.233 --> 00:02:01.993
Sweep the pH upward, and the predominant charge steps down: +1, 0, −1.

26
00:02:03.253 --> 00:02:04.893
Each drop is centred on a pKa.

27
00:02:06.293 --> 00:02:09.893
The exact zero crossing is the isoelectric point, the pI.

28
00:02:11.133 --> 00:02:17.153
For glycine, averaging its two pKa values gives the pI, about 5.97.

29
00:02:18.613 --> 00:02:24.073
In its simplest form, average the two pKa values around the ladder's net-zero rung.

30
00:02:25.017 --> 00:02:27.497
A side chain adds a third switch, or none.

31
00:02:28.957 --> 00:02:33.197
Glutamate's acid switch, near 4, has let go at blood pH: negative.

32
00:02:34.817 --> 00:02:38.077
Lysine's base switch, near 10.5, holds on: positive.

33
00:02:39.577 --> 00:02:41.837
Histidine's, near 6, is mostly neutral.

34
00:02:43.237 --> 00:02:46.057
Inside a protein, neighbours can shift these values.

35
00:02:46.057 --> 00:02:47.057
. . . .

36
00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:51.227
So list the switches, compare each pKa with the pH, and add.

37
00:02:52.487 --> 00:02:55.887
That sum is how sickle-cell hemoglobin was first told apart.

38
00:02:57.227 --> 00:03:00.107
A valine had replaced a glutamate on each beta chain.
